WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.502 --> 00:00:02.397
- Good morning believe nation
my name is Evan Carmichael,

00:00:02.397 --> 00:00:05.235
my one word is believe, and
I believe that entrepreneurs

00:00:05.235 --> 00:00:07.786
will solve all of the major
problems of the world.

00:00:07.786 --> 00:00:11.165
So today's message is be your real self.

00:00:11.165 --> 00:00:13.304
Over to you, Meryl Streep.

00:00:13.304 --> 00:00:15.484
(rooster crowing)

00:00:15.484 --> 00:00:18.030
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:20.585
♫ Entspresso keep my going

00:00:20.585 --> 00:00:24.085
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:25.294 --> 00:00:28.640
- I reached a point senior
year where my adjustment

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:31.487
felt like me, I had
actually convinced myself

00:00:31.487 --> 00:00:34.081
that I was this person and she me.

00:00:34.081 --> 00:00:36.998
Pretty, talented, but not stuck-up,

00:00:39.104 --> 00:00:41.701
a girl who laughed a lot at every

00:00:41.701 --> 00:00:44.034
stupid thing every boy said.

00:00:45.977 --> 00:00:49.887
And who lowered her
eyes at the right moment

00:00:49.887 --> 00:00:52.804
and deferred, who learned to deffer

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:57.837
when the boys took over the conversation.

00:00:57.837 --> 00:01:00.670
I really remember this so clearly.

00:01:02.939 --> 00:01:04.173
And I could tell it was working,

00:01:04.173 --> 00:01:07.419
I was much less annoying to
the guys than I had been.

00:01:07.419 --> 00:01:10.091
They liked me better and I liked that.

00:01:10.091 --> 00:01:14.375
This was conscious but
it was at the same time

00:01:14.375 --> 00:01:17.501
motivated and fully fully felt.

00:01:17.501 --> 00:01:19.751
This was real, real acting.

00:01:23.638 --> 00:01:26.521
I got to Vassar, which 43 years ago

00:01:26.521 --> 00:01:29.648
was a single sex institution like all the

00:01:29.648 --> 00:01:31.746
colleges in what they
called the seven sisters,

00:01:31.746 --> 00:01:33.579
the female Ivy League.

00:01:34.748 --> 00:01:37.093
And I made some very quick but lifelong

00:01:37.093 --> 00:01:39.093
and challenging friends.

00:01:40.429 --> 00:01:43.400
And with their help,
outside of any competition

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:46.439
for boys, my brain woke up.

00:01:46.439 --> 00:01:50.189
(audience laughs and cheers)

00:01:53.181 --> 00:01:56.538
I got up and I got outside myself

00:01:56.538 --> 00:02:00.569
and I found myself again,
I didn't have to pretend,

00:02:00.569 --> 00:02:05.002
I could be goofy, vehement,
aggressive and slovenly,

00:02:05.002 --> 00:02:08.962
and open and funny and
tough, my friends let me.

00:02:08.962 --> 00:02:11.063
I didn't wash my hair
for three weeks once.

00:02:11.063 --> 00:02:14.224
(audience laughs)

00:02:14.224 --> 00:02:17.807
They accepted me like
The Velveteen Rabbit.

00:02:19.041 --> 00:02:23.695
I became real instead of
an imaginary stuffed bunny.

00:02:23.695 --> 00:02:25.180
- What this video really
brings home for me

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:28.103
is how important the
environment you have is,

00:02:28.103 --> 00:02:30.732
the people around you,
how much they impact

00:02:30.732 --> 00:02:34.314
your beliefs, your
actions, the way you think,

00:02:34.314 --> 00:02:36.686
and just by being around different people

00:02:36.686 --> 00:02:38.666
you can become more of yourself.

00:02:38.666 --> 00:02:40.563
If you're around people who
are playing the big game

00:02:40.563 --> 00:02:42.295
you're going to want
to play a bigger game.

00:02:42.295 --> 00:02:44.019
If you're around people
who are thinking small

00:02:44.019 --> 00:02:45.459
and complaining, chances are you're

00:02:45.459 --> 00:02:47.969
going to think smaller
and complain a lot more.

00:02:47.969 --> 00:02:49.733
And so playing close
attention to the people

00:02:49.733 --> 00:02:51.174
who you are surrounding yourself with,

00:02:51.174 --> 00:02:52.250
really really really really really

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:54.684
really really really matters.

00:02:54.684 --> 00:02:56.737
There's the age old expression of you are

00:02:56.737 --> 00:02:58.058
the result of the five people who you

00:02:58.058 --> 00:03:00.634
hang out with, and it's true.

00:03:00.634 --> 00:03:02.463
So as a quick example
form my recent history,

00:03:02.463 --> 00:03:04.975
I came back from a
conference in Palm Springs

00:03:04.975 --> 00:03:06.852
called Climber Con which is for YouTubers

00:03:06.852 --> 00:03:09.154
with 100,000 plus subscribers and I met

00:03:09.154 --> 00:03:11.657
two interesting YouTubers who I had known

00:03:11.657 --> 00:03:13.565
before on email, but I got a
chance to interact with them.

00:03:13.565 --> 00:03:16.854
The first was Charlie
from Charisma on Command.

00:03:16.854 --> 00:03:18.039
He's got a great channel talking about

00:03:18.039 --> 00:03:19.765
how you can be more charismatic.

00:03:19.765 --> 00:03:21.371
One of the things that
Charlie really liked

00:03:21.371 --> 00:03:24.085
about me was my ability to network

00:03:24.085 --> 00:03:25.844
with people in the room
and make introductions

00:03:25.844 --> 00:03:27.903
with people to the point
where he made a video

00:03:27.903 --> 00:03:29.667
on his channel talking about it.

00:03:29.667 --> 00:03:32.009
- So last week I kind of had my mind blown

00:03:32.009 --> 00:03:33.450
and it's because I went to an event

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:35.381
and I learned more there about networking

00:03:35.381 --> 00:03:37.513
than I had learned in my entire life

00:03:37.513 --> 00:03:39.436
previous to that event.

00:03:39.436 --> 00:03:40.711
And what was really weird was that

00:03:40.711 --> 00:03:42.277
I learned it from an introvert.

00:03:42.277 --> 00:03:44.333
So I'd always expected that extroverts

00:03:44.333 --> 00:03:46.555
like myself would have
an easier time networking

00:03:46.555 --> 00:03:48.445
because we're more comfortable in groups,

00:03:48.445 --> 00:03:50.429
more comfortable talking
for long periods of time

00:03:50.429 --> 00:03:52.357
with strangers, but quite frankly this guy

00:03:52.357 --> 00:03:53.966
blew me out of the water.

00:03:53.966 --> 00:03:55.490
His network far exceeded mine.

00:03:55.490 --> 00:03:57.709
It felt like almost every time
that I mentioned something

00:03:57.709 --> 00:04:00.013
I was interested in, he's
like oh, yeah I know someone

00:04:00.013 --> 00:04:01.661
who can do that, I can
introduce you to them.

00:04:01.661 --> 00:04:03.308
And then after the event, he did.

00:04:03.308 --> 00:04:04.530
- One of the things that I loved about

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:05.887
Charlie was how healthy
he was and every time

00:04:05.887 --> 00:04:07.864
we went out he was ordering healthy foods,

00:04:07.864 --> 00:04:10.042
salads, salmon, and I'm sitting there

00:04:10.042 --> 00:04:12.423
eating pizza and junk.

00:04:12.423 --> 00:04:14.694
And so here's what would happen.

00:04:14.694 --> 00:04:16.419
If we spent more time together,

00:04:16.419 --> 00:04:18.153
if we were in close
proximity more than just

00:04:18.153 --> 00:04:20.046
once a year at this event,

00:04:20.046 --> 00:04:22.676
then I guarantee you
he would become better

00:04:22.676 --> 00:04:24.378
at networking and making introductions

00:04:24.378 --> 00:04:25.884
and talking to people,

00:04:25.884 --> 00:04:27.539
and I would start getting more fit

00:04:27.539 --> 00:04:29.928
and more healthy and more
conscious of what I was eating

00:04:29.928 --> 00:04:31.575
and working out more because we're

00:04:31.575 --> 00:04:33.055
in each other's environment.

00:04:33.055 --> 00:04:35.598
The second person is
Antonio who runs a channel

00:04:35.598 --> 00:04:37.531
called Real Men Real Style.

00:04:37.531 --> 00:04:39.969
And he was really impressed
with how I was pushing him

00:04:39.969 --> 00:04:41.943
to want to want to make a video a day.

00:04:41.943 --> 00:04:43.326
I said you got to make more
videos on your channel,

00:04:43.326 --> 00:04:44.601
if you want to stand out.

00:04:44.601 --> 00:04:46.266
YouTube wants a video a
day, you got to go daily

00:04:46.266 --> 00:04:48.774
on your channel, to the
point where he wanted

00:04:48.774 --> 00:04:50.457
to make a video about it as well.

00:04:50.457 --> 00:04:52.835
- And the one thing, the
one work I took from Evan

00:04:52.835 --> 00:04:56.135
is impact, this guy is wickedly smart.

00:04:56.135 --> 00:04:58.055
He's one of the few
people on there that I can

00:04:58.055 --> 00:04:59.860
be having a conversation with and realize

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:01.546
he's more intense than me,

00:05:01.546 --> 00:05:04.833
and he can turn the
situation so that you find

00:05:04.833 --> 00:05:06.230
you're agreeing with him.

00:05:06.230 --> 00:05:08.217
Literally he had me agreeing
that I should probably

00:05:08.217 --> 00:05:10.517
be putting out one video a day.

00:05:10.517 --> 00:05:11.813
- When I was hanging around Antonio

00:05:11.813 --> 00:05:13.627
I was always impressed by his style,

00:05:13.627 --> 00:05:15.846
and he was talking with me
about fragrances and smell.

00:05:15.846 --> 00:05:19.637
It starts to make me
think about what I should

00:05:19.637 --> 00:05:21.148
be doing more in my life.

00:05:21.148 --> 00:05:23.748
And so if we were spending
more time together

00:05:23.748 --> 00:05:26.287
in close proximity
beyond just once a year,

00:05:26.287 --> 00:05:29.403
he would be making more daily content.

00:05:29.403 --> 00:05:32.276
That would happen, I would
will it into existence for him.

00:05:32.276 --> 00:05:35.037
And he would be pushing
style advice on me,

00:05:35.037 --> 00:05:36.853
and fragrance and scent and all the things

00:05:36.853 --> 00:05:38.656
that he's working on,
and the more we spent

00:05:38.656 --> 00:05:40.589
time together the more we'd come together

00:05:40.589 --> 00:05:41.508
on those kinds of issues.

00:05:41.508 --> 00:05:43.358
And so the people that
you spend your time with

00:05:43.358 --> 00:05:45.248
really really really matter.

00:05:45.248 --> 00:05:46.764
Now how do you know if you
have the right environment?

00:05:46.764 --> 00:05:49.150
The answer is, start asking yourself about

00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:51.008
the dreams you have,
the big goals you have,

00:05:51.008 --> 00:05:52.405
the mission that you're on, the big thing

00:05:52.405 --> 00:05:54.049
that you want to accomplish.

00:05:54.049 --> 00:05:56.266
What's the reaction of
the people close to you

00:05:56.266 --> 00:05:58.154
when you tell them your plans?

00:05:58.154 --> 00:06:00.002
Here's my dream of what I want to be,

00:06:00.002 --> 00:06:02.423
mom and dad, best friend,

00:06:02.423 --> 00:06:04.923
teacher, professor, neighbors.

00:06:06.172 --> 00:06:07.405
What's the reaction?

00:06:07.405 --> 00:06:08.635
Are people supportive?

00:06:08.635 --> 00:06:10.359
Are people encouraging?

00:06:10.359 --> 00:06:11.969
Are people trying to offer resources

00:06:11.969 --> 00:06:13.812
and help on ideas to make it better?

00:06:13.812 --> 00:06:17.185
Are people apathetic and they don't care?

00:06:17.185 --> 00:06:20.438
Are people negative and telling
you that you can't do it?

00:06:20.438 --> 00:06:22.325
Are you even afraid just to bring up

00:06:22.325 --> 00:06:24.137
the subject to them because you know

00:06:24.137 --> 00:06:27.002
what they're immediate
reaction is going to be?

00:06:27.002 --> 00:06:29.063
If that's the environment that you're in,

00:06:29.063 --> 00:06:30.542
you need to switch.

00:06:30.542 --> 00:06:32.759
Right, if you are afraid
to bring up your ideas

00:06:32.759 --> 00:06:34.567
to the people around you because you know

00:06:34.567 --> 00:06:35.809
it's just going to be negativity,

00:06:35.809 --> 00:06:38.201
then you need to switch your environment.

00:06:38.201 --> 00:06:39.762
As long as you're around those people

00:06:39.762 --> 00:06:41.900
you will never do great things.

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:43.019
And then you might say but Evan,

00:06:43.019 --> 00:06:44.697
I don't know great people, I don't know

00:06:44.697 --> 00:06:47.137
how to change my environment,
I'm stuck with these people,

00:06:47.137 --> 00:06:49.150
it's my family, it's my friends.

00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:51.162
So you're right to some extent,

00:06:51.162 --> 00:06:52.722
they're your family, they're your friends.

00:06:52.722 --> 00:06:54.324
One, you can limit your exposure,

00:06:54.324 --> 00:06:55.933
you don't have to spend
as much time with them.

00:06:55.933 --> 00:06:58.039
You can only talk about
certain things with them.

00:06:58.039 --> 00:07:00.421
And then two, you can
design the environment,

00:07:00.421 --> 00:07:03.180
and maybe going to events,
finding places like

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:05.050
Climber Con at least for me as a YouTuber.

00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:07.213
Finding other events, finding local

00:07:07.213 --> 00:07:08.056
things that you don't have to travel

00:07:08.056 --> 00:07:10.071
and spend a lot of money to go to.

00:07:10.071 --> 00:07:12.374
Finding local people,
clubs, meetup groups,

00:07:12.374 --> 00:07:14.308
like-minded people to hang out with

00:07:14.308 --> 00:07:15.461
and spend time with.

00:07:15.461 --> 00:07:16.736
It could be aspirational mentor,

00:07:16.736 --> 00:07:18.963
that's where I learn
most of my stuff from.

00:07:18.963 --> 00:07:20.651
The tops tens of people who I look up to

00:07:20.651 --> 00:07:22.170
who've done amazing things.

00:07:22.170 --> 00:07:24.244
The more time you spend hanging around

00:07:24.244 --> 00:07:26.650
people like Steve Jobs and Elon Musk

00:07:26.650 --> 00:07:28.361
and Oprah Winfrey and
listening to their advice

00:07:28.361 --> 00:07:29.639
and how they think and how they talk

00:07:29.639 --> 00:07:31.320
and how they deal with situations,

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:34.251
the more time you spend
hanging around them

00:07:34.251 --> 00:07:36.056
and the less time you spend hanging around

00:07:36.056 --> 00:07:37.948
these negative people,

00:07:37.948 --> 00:07:39.469
the more you're going to be like them.

00:07:39.469 --> 00:07:40.954
You're going to gravitate there,

00:07:40.954 --> 00:07:42.437
you're going to believe in your big ideas,

00:07:42.437 --> 00:07:43.834
you're going to accomplish bigger things,

00:07:43.834 --> 00:07:45.407
you're pulled in that direction.

00:07:45.407 --> 00:07:49.212
Your environment is so so so so important.

00:07:49.212 --> 00:07:50.655
And so the question of the day today is,

00:07:50.655 --> 00:07:52.783
I'm curious what does your
environment look like?

00:07:52.783 --> 00:07:55.541
Are they full of positive
optimistic people helping you?

00:07:55.541 --> 00:07:57.552
Apathetic people who don't really care?

00:07:57.552 --> 00:08:00.226
Or negative people who tell
you that you can't do it?

00:08:00.226 --> 00:08:01.256
Really curious to find out,

00:08:01.256 --> 00:08:02.853
leave it down in the comments below,

00:08:02.853 --> 00:08:04.172
thank you for sharing.

00:08:04.172 --> 00:08:05.483
I also want to give a quick shout out

00:08:05.483 --> 00:08:07.789
to Susana Villalobos,
thank you so much Susana

00:08:07.789 --> 00:08:10.614
for picking up a copy of
my book, Your One Word.

00:08:10.614 --> 00:08:12.334
It really really means a lot to me

00:08:12.334 --> 00:08:14.066
and I hope you're enjoying the read.

00:08:14.066 --> 00:08:15.247
So thank you guys again for watching,

00:08:15.247 --> 00:08:17.180
I believe in you, I hope you continue

00:08:17.180 --> 00:08:20.682
to believe in yourself and
whatever your one word is.

00:08:20.682 --> 00:08:22.236
And I'll see you again tomorrow morning

00:08:22.236 --> 00:08:24.499
for another shot of Entspresso.

00:08:24.499 --> 00:08:27.217
♫ I wake up every morning

00:08:27.217 --> 00:08:30.800
♫ Entspresso keep me going

00:08:33.711 --> 00:08:35.606
- Well I haven't had any regrets,

00:08:35.606 --> 00:08:37.741
and it's sometimes really hard.

00:08:37.741 --> 00:08:40.289
I'll tell you a funny story,

00:08:40.289 --> 00:08:44.523
I remember right before
I got A Different World,

00:08:44.523 --> 00:08:46.825
I auditioned for Blossom.

00:08:46.825 --> 00:08:47.779
You remember the TV show Blossom?

00:08:47.779 --> 00:08:48.812
- Oh absolutely, yes.

00:08:48.812 --> 00:08:51.687
- Do you remember her
friend the young girl,

00:08:51.687 --> 00:08:54.687
her Six, I auditioned for Six, okay?

00:08:55.962 --> 00:08:57.738
And I had my hair up in a little pony tail

00:08:57.738 --> 00:08:58.722
with this little jumpsuit,

00:08:58.722 --> 00:09:00.827
I looked like I was about 12.

00:09:00.827 --> 00:09:03.057
Got the role, okay?

00:09:03.057 --> 00:09:05.075
Got the role to play Six.

00:09:05.075 --> 00:09:08.742
And I was like, I was
18, 19 years old then.

00:09:09.643 --> 00:09:12.291
I looked to my agent Nancy Rainford,

00:09:12.291 --> 00:09:15.255
I said Nancy I can't do this.

00:09:15.255 --> 00:09:18.575
I just can't do it, I'm
going to be miserable

00:09:18.575 --> 00:09:20.926
playing a 12 year old on this show.

00:09:20.926 --> 00:09:22.692
I can't do this.

00:09:22.692 --> 00:09:26.692
And we had to turn it
down, and let me tell you,

00:09:28.284 --> 00:09:29.381
them folks was not happy.

00:09:29.381 --> 00:09:32.468
It's like who does this girl think she is,

00:09:32.468 --> 00:09:34.777
you know, that whole thing.

00:09:34.777 --> 00:09:37.905
But within two weeks I
got A Different World,

00:09:37.905 --> 00:09:41.528
and that really taught me a lesson

00:09:41.528 --> 00:09:43.945
which was to really listen to

00:09:47.116 --> 00:09:48.675
and follow my gut,

00:09:48.675 --> 00:09:51.722
because everything that shines isn't gold,

00:09:51.722 --> 00:09:53.241
you know what I mean?

00:09:53.241 --> 00:09:55.623
And so it really taught me a very valuable

00:09:55.623 --> 00:09:58.090
lesson to just stay true to myself.

00:09:58.090 --> 00:10:00.146
If there's nothing that I haven't learned

00:10:00.146 --> 00:10:04.336
about this business, it's
really about staying true

00:10:04.336 --> 00:10:08.503
to why I'm here, why I started
acting in the first place,

00:10:10.956 --> 00:10:13.831
and what's important to me.

00:10:13.831 --> 00:10:18.060
And keeping my artistic
integrity as well as my creative,

00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:22.227
my personal integrity, and
that was a big lesson for me.

00:10:23.083 --> 00:10:25.217
And let me tell you, that was not an easy

00:10:25.217 --> 00:10:28.178
thing to turn down, being a young actress

00:10:28.178 --> 00:10:32.871
you go this opportunity
for this series regular,

00:10:32.871 --> 00:10:34.621
it's money, you know?

00:10:35.544 --> 00:10:37.184
But I just felt deep down inside,

00:10:37.184 --> 00:10:40.102
I was like something's coming
your way, just fall back.

00:10:40.102 --> 00:10:43.144
And then I got to meet Debbie Allen, yeah.

00:10:43.144 --> 00:10:45.618
(audience applause)

00:10:45.618 --> 00:10:49.020
- I said look, why don't we change

00:10:49.020 --> 00:10:51.209
everything opposite Bruce Lee?

00:10:51.209 --> 00:10:54.009
Be myself, just me, Jackie Chan.

00:10:54.009 --> 00:10:56.315
- That takes a lot of
confidence and belief.

00:10:56.315 --> 00:11:00.630
- And also I really think you,
the producer will see that.

00:11:00.630 --> 00:11:03.467
He said okay, do whatever you like to do.

00:11:03.467 --> 00:11:05.884
Change, no Bruce Lee, comedy.

00:11:07.010 --> 00:11:08.990
When Bruce Lee kick high, I kick low.

00:11:08.990 --> 00:11:13.351
When Bruce Lee after
punch, ugh, I do bah, ow.

00:11:13.351 --> 00:11:15.533
Just like normal human being,

00:11:15.533 --> 00:11:17.722
normal when I was young,
I'm fighting on the street,

00:11:17.722 --> 00:11:19.534
one by one boom, I knocked somebody down,

00:11:19.534 --> 00:11:21.020
two by one, I knocked somebody down,

00:11:21.020 --> 00:11:22.717
three, I run away.

00:11:22.717 --> 00:11:24.654
So in the movie, do the same thing.

00:11:24.654 --> 00:11:28.611
Everything used in the
movie, it's my real life.

00:11:28.611 --> 00:11:30.170
- [Presenter] When she was 17 years old

00:11:30.170 --> 00:11:31.526
Stephanie got her high-school diploma

00:11:31.526 --> 00:11:33.379
and left the nuns to join the prestigious

00:11:33.379 --> 00:11:36.098
Tisch school of arts, a school entirely

00:11:36.098 --> 00:11:37.948
dedicated to artistic disciplines.

00:11:37.948 --> 00:11:40.371
She took numerous classes
such as art story,

00:11:40.371 --> 00:11:42.338
music, theater, and dancing.

00:11:42.338 --> 00:11:44.645
I was really blessed and lucky to get in,

00:11:44.645 --> 00:11:47.062
I got in very early, I was 17

00:11:48.724 --> 00:11:51.807
and I was one of 20 people in America

00:11:53.170 --> 00:11:55.753
that got into the school early.

00:11:56.842 --> 00:11:59.141
And I was really happy and excited

00:11:59.141 --> 00:12:01.400
and I just felt like all
my dreams were coming true.

00:12:01.400 --> 00:12:05.567
And it was a wonderful school
but it was very factory-like,

00:12:07.291 --> 00:12:09.640
and not in the Warhol factory-like way.

00:12:09.640 --> 00:12:12.292
More like a sausage link factory

00:12:12.292 --> 00:12:14.753
where everyone was taught the same sorts

00:12:14.753 --> 00:12:18.940
of things and I think what I
didn't like about this school

00:12:18.940 --> 00:12:22.278
was I felt that they wanted
us all to be the same

00:12:22.278 --> 00:12:24.624
and possess the same skills

00:12:24.624 --> 00:12:28.624
and I had something very
different inside of me.

